MLLM description: The Mourning Dove is a small to medium-sized bird with a slender body, long tail, and short, pointed beak. Its plumage is predominantly light brown or grayish-brown, with darker brown streaks on the back and wings. The underparts are lighter, often appearing pale pink or buff-colored. A distinctive feature is the black "eye spot" on the side of its head, which is surrounded by a white ring. The bird's legs and feet are pinkish-red, and it has a small, sharp beak.

Behaviorally, Mourning Doves are known for their gentle and quiet demeanor. They are often seen foraging on the ground for seeds, grains, and insects. They are also known for their mournful call, which gives them their common name. 

In terms of size, Mourning Doves are relatively small, typically measuring around 7-8 inches in length. They have a wingspan of about 12-14 inches.

The distinctive physical features, color patterns, and markings, along with their size and proportions, make Mourning Doves easily recognizable. Their unique identifying trait is the black "eye spot" on the side of their head, which is a key characteristic used for fine-grained visual recognition
