MLLM description: The Red-winged Blackbird is a medium-sized bird with a robust body and short tail. Its most distinctive feature is the bright red patch on its shoulder, which is visible in males during breeding season. The female and juvenile birds lack this red patch and have a more subdued coloration.

In terms of size, the Red-winged Blackbird is approximately 7-8 inches long, with a wingspan of about 10-12 inches. They have a dark brown head and back, with a lighter underside. The wings are dark with white wing bars, and the tail is short and square-shaped.

Behaviorally, Red-winged Blackbirds are known for their territorial behavior, especially during the breeding season when males establish territories and defend them against intruders. They are also known to engage in aggressive displays, such as wing-flapping and bill-clattering, to assert dominance over rivals.

The Red-winged Blackbird's unique identifying traits include its red shoulder patch, dark plumage, and short, square tail. These features help distinguish it from other blackbirds, such as the Common Grackle or the Cowbird, which do not possess the same distinct markings.
